From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 07:12:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] engine: scroll the ghostty pane from the scrollbar

UserScrollViewport was a stub, so dragging the scrollbar on a ghostty
pane did nothing - one of Phase 5's exit criteria, silently unmet.

WT hands over an absolute buffer row for the top of the viewport, and
ghostty's scroll_to_row takes an absolute row with 0 being the first row
of the screen (scrollback included), so the two line up with no
arithmetic in between. Driven through the binding-action API because that
is the only route to an absolute position; the mouse-scroll entry point
moves by deltas and would drift against the thumb over a long drag.

Verified in the same session, from traces rather than by eye:
- tab title: `cmd /k title X` reaches WT's window title, i.e. child OSC
  -> write_pty_output -> ghostty parse -> SET_TITLE -> TitleChanged.
- resize: growing the window moved the grid 53x29 -> 53x34 and pushed it
  to the ConPTY.
- side-by-side: a ghostty pane split with a cascadia pane gives one
  window, two ConPTYs, two shells, no crash.

Not verified: the scrollbar drag itself, which needs a hand on a mouse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ee27Fih3vSPYsqrUBpUoc
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 20 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index e3d887d06..d37aa06d7 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -713,8 +713,26 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         return false;
     }
 
-    void GhosttyControlCore::UserScrollViewport(const int32_t /*viewTop*/)
+    // The scrollbar's thumb, dragged. WT gives an absolute buffer row for the
+    // top of the viewport, which is exactly what ghostty's scroll_to_row takes
+    // (0 being the first row of the screen, scrollback included), so the two
+    // coordinate systems line up without arithmetic.
+    //
+    // Driven through the binding-action API because that is the only route to
+    // an absolute scroll position; the mouse-scroll entry point only moves by
+    // deltas and would drift against the thumb.
+    void GhosttyControlCore::UserScrollViewport(const int32_t viewTop)
     {
+        if (!_surface)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
+        const auto action = fmt::format("scroll_to_row:{}", std::max(0, viewTop));
+        if (!ghostty_surface_binding_action(_surface, action.data(), action.size()))
+        {
+            _trace(L"scroll_to_row rejected (%hs)", action.c_str());
+        }
     }
 
 #pragma endregion
